  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>We know that shortest distance between two skew lines exists along the line which is perpendicular to both the lines.</p><p>Now finding the equation of a plane&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mfenced></math>, whose normal is perpendicular to the given first line and the line obtained by planes&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.</p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> is:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p>It should be parallel to given line.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math><br />Thus, Plane,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>is</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/d73de4df-2d6e-43a8-a303-4f455e0dd47b-image.png\" style=\"width: 307px; height: 240px;\" /></p><p>So, the shortest distance between the lines will be same as&nbsp;distance between the plane&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math>&nbsp;and given line&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>0</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mi>z</mi></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mfrac></math></p>Shortest distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo></math> Perpendicular distance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math> from this plane <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>|</mo></mrow><msqrt><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>",
